        "object": "A damaged home in the Sheikh Maqsoud neighborhood of Aleppo.&nbsp; (Mauricio Centurion) via The Nation Once a month, we put together a list of stories weâ€™ve been reading: news you mightâ€™ve missed or crucial conversations going on around the web. We focus on environmental justice, radical municipalism, new politics, political theory, and resources for action and education. We try to include articles that have been published recently but will last, that are relatively light and inspiring, and are from corners of the web that donâ€™t always get the light of day. This will also be a space to keep you up to date with news about whatâ€™s happening at Uneven Earth. February was short, so we decided to do a combined February-March newsletter â€“ and itâ€™s a good one! We collected stories on Syria, Turkey & Rojava, the Cop City movement in Atlanta, and false climate solutions; a critique of Elon Muskâ€™s Twitter; a discussion of the sustainability of bicycles; and a reflection on the importance of redefining â€˜abundanceâ€™, to name a few. We also included a climate action toolkit for tired people, because thatâ€™s most of us in this vast capitalist hellscape. If you find these lists useful, you can support us by sharing them on social media and with your friends and family! A small note that the articles linked in this newsletter do not represent the views of Uneven Earth. When reading, please keep in mind that we donâ€™t have capacity to do further research on the authors or publishers! Uneven Earth updates Stories of permafrost | A call to look beyond permanence LÃ¼tzerath bleibt! | At the edge of the 1.5Â°C frontier Top 5 articles to read Climate in 2023: escalation and backlash How to meet the climate crisis? Redefine â€˜ƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;r5qzWQyxZjfCiQUQMKiEqJg2Z5lZmGO46aQXBFTwECcƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstardunit‚dmarkgcurrentƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEn